@@s001 Polynomials: models linear in the parameters
@@s002 no:curved in x,ok:straight in w
@@s003 dato:the data,ora:the row being built,ok:X w = y
@@s004 dato:the 3 points,ok:the exact parabola
@@s005 ok:parabola through 3 points,no:the point that does not match,due:least squares
@@s006 dato:3 points,ok:c = 0,ora:c = 0.5,due:c = −0.5
@@s007 dato:the data,ora:the row being built,ok:the weights
@@s008 Polynomials: models linear in the parameters
@@s009 On the slides the question is: "Why is a polynomial a non-linear function but can be written as an inner product?" The answer is the central idea of weeks 4 and 5: a model can be <b>curved in x</b> but <b>linear in the weights w</b>, and then it is learned with linear algebra. Here: the design matrix and the three cases: as many points as parameters, more, fewer.
@@s010 Curved in x, linear in the weights
@@s011 A polynomial of degree 2, <span class="m">y = w₀ + w₁x + w₂x²</span>, draws a curve: it is <b>not linear in x</b> (doubling x does not double y).
@@s012 But in training x is a known datum and the unknowns are the weights. With respect to the weights the polynomial is a <b>sum of weights times known numbers</b> (1, x, x²): <b>it is linear in w</b>. In the form of an inner product, <span class="m">y = [w₀, w₁, w₂] · [1, x, x²]</span>.
@@s013 y = w₀ + w₁x + w₂x²
@@s014 A polynomial of degree 2.
@@s015 It is an <a href="w02-prodotto-interno.html">inner product</a> between the weights and a vector of "features" built from x.
@@s016 in x: <mark class="no">not linear</mark> (x² is a curve) in w: <mark class="ok">linear</mark>
@@s017 If I double the weights, y doubles: with respect to w it is a linear combination. And w is what we want to learn.
@@s018 The same technique as the line through two points (<a href="w03-sistemi-lineari.html">linear systems</a>), with more columns.
@@s019 The same formula: curved in x, straight in w₂
@@s020 The design matrix
@@s021 With N points and a model linear in the parameters, each point gives an equation. Together they are written <span class="m">Xw = y</span>, where X is the <b>design matrix</b>: <b>one row per point</b>, <b>one column per parameter</b>. Column j contains the number that multiplies wⱼ in each equation.
@@s022 From N points to a matrix
@@s023 For polynomials it is called a Vandermonde matrix (<code>np.vander(x, d+1, increasing=True)</code>).
@@s024 X: transformed data; w: weights; y: observed outputs.
@@s025 The order of the coefficients
@@s026 <code>np.vander(x, 3, increasing=True)</code> puts the columns in the order 1, x, x², and the weights come out as [w₀, w₁, w₂]. <code>np.polyfit</code> instead returns the coefficients from the <b>highest</b> degree: for the same 4 points of the section below it gives [0.5, 0.9, 0.9] instead of [0.9, 0.9, 0.5]. Same numbers, reversed order (verified).
@@s027 Three points, three rows
@@s028 As many points as parameters: the exact solution
@@s029 If the number of points equals the number of parameters, X is <b>square</b>. If the points have all different x, X is invertible and the system has one single solution: <span class="m">w = X⁻¹y</span>, in practice <code>np.linalg.solve(X, y)</code>.
@@s030 The model found passes <b>exactly</b> through all the points. For two points it is the line of week 1, for three points a parabola, in general a polynomial of degree N − 1 for N points: it is exercise 13 of the test (<a href="nozioni-esercizi-test.html">exercises by hand</a>).
@@s031 (0, 1), (1, 2), (2, 5): y = 1 + x²
@@s032 More points than parameters: the overdetermined system
@@s033 With real data the points are almost always <b>many more</b> than the parameters, and because of the noise no model passes through all of them. X is tall and narrow (N × p with N &gt; p), the system is <b>overdetermined</b> and has no exact solution.
@@s034 The question then changes: not "which w makes all the equations true", but "which w makes them <b>nearly true</b>, getting it wrong as little as possible". With the least squares loss the answer is unique and is computed with <code>np.linalg.lstsq</code>; the formula and the geometry are in week 5 (<a href="w05-minimi-quadrati.html">least squares</a>). It is the goal of week 4: "relate overdetermined systems of equations to least squares optimization".
@@s035 A fourth point: no exact parabola, the best approximate one
@@s036 Fewer points than parameters: infinitely many solutions
@@s037 The opposite case: more parameters than points. The system is <b>underdetermined</b> and has <b>infinitely many</b> solutions: many different models pass exactly through the data, and the data give no way to choose one.
@@s038 The problem shows as soon as a new prediction is made: models equally perfect on the data give very different answers outside. It is the extreme form of overfitting: a model too flexible for the available data.
@@s039 Model
@@s040 Parameters
@@s041 Row of the design matrix
@@s042 Minimum points
@@s043 line y = w₀ + w₁x
@@s044 parabola
@@s045 polynomial of degree d
@@s046 plane in 3D y = w₀ + w₁x₁ + w₂x₂
@@s047 hyperplane in n dimensions
@@s048 Exam question Q2 (b)
@@s049 "Explain how linear and certain non-linear models can be learned by solving systems of linear equations… Discuss how model complexity, such as polynomial degree, affects the number of parameters and therefore the amount of training data required." Higher degree ⇒ more parameters ⇒ more points needed; and with too many parameters relative to the data the model chases the noise (overfitting, week 7).
@@s050 Three points, infinitely many cubics
@@s051 More features: the same design matrix
@@s052 Everything that holds for polynomials also holds for models with more features. For the plane <span class="m">y = w₀ + w₁x₁ + w₂x₂</span> the row of the design matrix is [1, x₁, x₂]; for a hyperplane in n dimensions [1, x₁, …, xₙ].
@@s053 The general rule: the columns of the design matrix can be <b>any known function</b> of the features (x₁, x₁², x₁·x₂, sin x₁, …). As long as the model is "weights for known columns, then sum", it is linear in the parameters and it is learned by solving Xw = y, exactly or with least squares. Choosing the right columns is the <b>transformation of the features</b> of week 5.
@@s054 A plane from three points: X = [1, x₁, x₂]
